summary:
Taliv Elevator Services, Inc. (client) is a New York elevator maintenance contractor. Under a Component Supply Agreement effective February 1, 2024, it buys replacement elevator components (door operators, controllers, traction machine parts, safety gear) from Breiraind Lift Components Corp. Taliv's parent, Taliv Holdings LLC, guarantees Taliv's payment obligations. On October 20, 2026 Taliv sent Breiraind a notice letter. The letter elects not to renew the agreement and reports that Taliv Holdings has signed a Membership Interest Purchase Agreement to sell itself to Pennock Vertical Partners, with closing expected December 15, 2026. The partner wants an issue-spotting memo. The folder contains the supply agreement, a data processing addendum, the notice letter, the order form, the parent guaranty and a quality agreement, plus QBR meeting notes and a certificate of insurance. Five problems are planted:
- exclusivity that, through the order form's definition of Serviced Units, reaches all affiliates across the US and Canada;
- a warranty remedy gutted by the quality agreement's narrow definition of Nonconforming Product;
- a change-of-control consent right triggered by the parent-level sale, with no consent sought;
- a non-renewal notice sent after the 120-day window closed;
- a supplier assignment right with no restriction.
